WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:02.800
Film Courage: When you sit down to write a
story do you begin with an outlining?

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:09.860
Van Ditthavong, Filmmaker/Photographer: Every
single script has been different.

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:20.660
The last one I just did ALL ROADS TO PEARLA
I did outline.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:33.830
The last one I just did is called ONLY THE
DEAD LEAVE EARLY I did not outline at all.

00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:45.330
I just started with an image and then locked
myself up, locked myself in a car.

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:53.620
I would drive my wife to work and then I would
sit in the car because that would force me

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:57.610
not to look at my phone and I would write
for a couple of hours.

00:00:57.610 --> 00:00:59.830
Then that way I could talk things out.

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:04.660
So if you’re walking by and seeing this
guy talking to himself in a car he’s either

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:10.841
gone crazy or something but I felt that was
right for that particular script and that

00:01:10.841 --> 00:01:14.120
made me do it every day something that made
me do it.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:20.990
Another script prior to was a different process
so it just changes.

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:26.960
I think that might be international because
I want to try different ways but what kind

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:32.350
of helped me was maybe hearing Jeff Nichols
talk about movie days and that gave me a structure

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:40.330
like Movie Day 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7 if the
movie took place in seven days that gave me

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:46.770
a feel of what is going to happen during that
time with these characters.

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:51.680
I think it depends for each one but I think
the outline does help.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:56.480
Sometimes I know the ending prior to so I
will work backwards and if I ever get stuck

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:00.920
I will jump different places and jump different
scenes.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:07.170
I usually have an image to start out with
and then something to end with and I’ll

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:15.420
build and then eliminate and form up the story
there but I try to lead with the characters

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:21.349
and once I have a solid grip and understanding
the characters sort of let them go in the

00:02:21.349 --> 00:02:22.349
story.

00:02:22.349 --> 00:02:26.209
Film Courage: What was the image for ALL ROADS
TO PEARLA?

00:02:26.209 --> 00:02:41.689
Van: Wow…you know at first it was…a plastic
bag hanging in a tree and near a nodding donkey

00:02:41.689 --> 00:02:47.870
just going back and forth and the sound of
the chain (tick, tick, tick) and then it was

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:55.019
just the landscape and I remember I did a
mood reel six years ago showing what type

00:02:55.019 --> 00:03:01.180
of film I would want (I would cut that piece
together) and I put that piece with Daniel

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:03.499
Johnson’s song Deviltown.

00:03:03.499 --> 00:03:13.169
It’s funny to see that mood reel six years
ago and then the trailer now how close it

00:03:13.169 --> 00:03:14.169
is.

00:03:14.169 --> 00:03:24.559
It’s kind of cool that the world still maintained
throughout through the process so I like that.

00:03:24.559 --> 00:03:32.870
But the image was this plastic bag that just
was stuck in a tree.

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:33.370
Question For The Viewers: Do you think of
“movie days” when you are writing?

